WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.487
What if working with an AI began with a

2
00:00:02.487 --> 00:00:05.818
conversation? You speak, it listens, and an answer comes

3
00:00:05.818 --> 00:00:08.341
back as speech. Korina is a local first project

4
00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.189
that connects those pieces.

5
00:00:10.098 --> 00:00:13.106
Its repository brings together a browser interface, a Python

6
00:00:13.106 --> 00:00:17.062
backend, speech recognition, a response model, and a configurable

7
00:00:17.062 --> 00:00:17.607
speech service.

8
00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:20.700
The interesting part is how the work is divided.

9
00:00:21.466 --> 00:00:24.108
Korina Converse handles the conversation you experience in the

10
00:00:24.108 --> 00:00:27.255
moment. A background agent receives transcript updates and can

11
00:00:27.255 --> 00:00:29.433
return context or a higher priority message.

12
00:00:30.018 --> 00:00:31.954
The two loops have different jobs, while sharing the

13
00:00:31.954 --> 00:00:32.962
same ongoing conversation.

14
00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:36.195
Over the next ten minutes, we will follow a

15
00:00:36.195 --> 00:00:38.392
spoken turn through that system, look at how the

16
00:00:38.392 --> 00:00:40.831
agent contributes, and explore the code that makes the

17
00:00:40.831 --> 00:00:44.178
parts replaceable. This explanation follows the repository as it

18
00:00:44.178 --> 00:00:46.899
exists today, including the places where the intended design

19
00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:47.625
is still developing.

20
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.651
Local first describes where the core experience can run.

21
00:00:54.114 --> 00:00:55.766
The browser talks to Korina on your machine.

22
00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.908
A local speech recognizer can turn microphone audio into

23
00:00:58.908 --> 00:01:01.446
text, a local language model can generate a response,

24
00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:03.480
and a local speech engine can speak it.

25
00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:07.146
These are separate services connected into a usable conversation.

26
00:01:08.321 --> 00:01:10.112
That separation also gives you choices.

27
00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:12.405
The response model may come from a local server

28
00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.015
or an OpenAI compatible endpoint.

29
00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:16.027
Speech settings have their own controls.

30
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.159
An agent can use a different model from the

31
00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:18.944
one speaking to you.

32
00:01:19.407 --> 00:01:21.841
Choosing a remote endpoint changes where that particular request

33
00:01:21.841 --> 00:01:22.263
is sent.

34
00:01:23.156 --> 00:01:25.531
Think of Korina as the coordinating layer around those

35
00:01:25.531 --> 00:01:28.429
components. It helps them agree on configuration, move information

36
00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:31.126
between stages, and present the interaction in one place.

37
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.641
Local first is a useful starting point for control

38
00:01:33.641 --> 00:01:36.640
and experimentation; the actual data path follows the providers

39
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.646
and endpoints you configure.

40
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.514
Now follow the first stage: listening.

41
00:01:43.062 --> 00:01:45.493
The browser captures microphone audio and estimates when speech

42
00:01:45.493 --> 00:01:48.289
is happening. Korina uses an adaptive energy based voice

43
00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:51.121
activity detector. Its endpointing settings decide how much

44
00:01:51.121 --> 00:01:52.894
silence is enough to treat an utterance as finished,

45
00:01:53.187 --> 00:01:55.416
with different defaults for conversation and longer dictation.

46
00:01:56.268 --> 00:01:58.880
While you are speaking, rolling audio windows can enter

47
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.067
a partial transcription queue.

48
00:02:00.614 --> 00:02:02.824
Those windows are queued rather than simply discarded when

49
00:02:02.824 --> 00:02:05.600
recognition takes longer. The interface can therefore build up

50
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.303
a transcript during the turn, while finalization determines what

51
00:02:08.303 --> 00:02:09.435
is delivered when you finish speaking.

52
00:02:10.415 --> 00:02:12.701
The stable recognition path uses faster whisper.

53
00:02:13.268 --> 00:02:15.866
There is also an experimental path for compatible multimodal

54
00:02:15.866 --> 00:02:19.341
audio models. This distinction matters: selecting an audio capable

55
00:02:19.341 --> 00:02:20.640
model is only part of the problem.

56
00:02:21.335 --> 00:02:23.640
The serving endpoint must support the expected audio request

57
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.320
as well, which is why capability checks and fallback

58
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:27.802
behavior appear elsewhere in the stack.

59
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.438
Once a turn is ready, its transcript reaches the

60
00:02:33.438 --> 00:02:36.265
response model. The main chat route sends the conversation

61
00:02:36.265 --> 00:02:39.053
to the configured language model endpoint and receives an

62
00:02:39.053 --> 00:02:42.021
answer. Korina can work with local serving tools such

63
00:02:42.021 --> 00:02:46.393
as llama.cpp, LM Studio, and Ollama, alongside a generic

64
00:02:46.393 --> 00:02:47.075
compatible endpoint.

65
00:02:48.247 --> 00:02:50.970
Conversation history gives the model context for what you

66
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.013
just said. Agent reports can contribute additional context to

67
00:02:54.013 --> 00:02:55.955
a later reply, depending on the coordination settings.

68
00:02:56.535 --> 00:02:58.277
This lets the speaking model stay focused on the

69
00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:00.779
exchange while useful background information arrives through a

70
00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:01.079
separate path.

71
00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.537
The returned text still has to become something suitable

72
00:03:04.537 --> 00:03:07.845
for speech. The frontend removes transcript labels and hidden

73
00:03:07.845 --> 00:03:10.030
thinking blocks before requesting spoken output.

74
00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:13.298
That small boundary has a practical purpose: an internal

75
00:03:13.298 --> 00:03:16.145
acknowledgement label or reasoning marker should not become part

76
00:03:16.145 --> 00:03:17.528
of the sentence you hear through your speakers.

77
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.102
Korina uses a configurable text to speech service to

78
00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:23.932
turn the answer into audio.

79
00:03:24.573 --> 00:03:26.855
The repository includes a Kokoro streaming server, with a

80
00:03:26.855 --> 00:03:27.912
buffered wave file fallback.

81
00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:29.704
In the browser, Web Audio handles playback.

82
00:03:30.552 --> 00:03:32.929
The engine used to narrate this explainer is Ming 3;

83
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.777
that is a production choice for this video.

84
00:03:35.812 --> 00:03:37.924
A conversation also needs room for interruption.

85
00:03:38.546 --> 00:03:40.753
Korina has browser side barge in logic and controls

86
00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:43.204
for deciding when fresh microphone input should count as

87
00:03:43.204 --> 00:03:45.335
a real turn. The aim is a responsive exchange

88
00:03:45.335 --> 00:03:47.786
where you can continue talking, rather than waiting passively

89
00:03:47.786 --> 00:03:49.445
for every generated sentence to finish.

90
00:03:50.405 --> 00:03:53.653
Then there are acknowledgement phrases: short cached audio clips

91
00:03:53.653 --> 00:03:55.617
that can signal thinking or idle activity.

92
00:03:56.145 --> 00:03:57.977
Their manifest holds the source text and tags.

93
00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:00.073
Generated audio is treated as cache.

94
00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:03.302
The interface labels acknowledgements visibly while keeping those

95
00:04:03.302 --> 00:04:05.682
labels out of model history, helping prevent the conversation

96
00:04:05.682 --> 00:04:07.608
from feeding its own housekeeping back into itself.

97
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.035
The background agent runs alongside the human facing loop.

98
00:04:13.441 --> 00:04:15.597
Korina delivers transcript updates through an agent interface.

99
00:04:16.056 --> 00:04:18.159
The agent maintains compact state and emits events or

100
00:04:18.159 --> 00:04:20.544
reports. Converse then decides how to present those contributions.

101
00:04:21.551 --> 00:04:24.042
The current repository emphasizes this reporting and coordination

102
00:04:24.042 --> 00:04:27.081
behavior; a broader execution runtime is still a development

103
00:04:27.081 --> 00:04:27.434
direction.

104
00:04:28.414 --> 00:04:30.371
A normal report can wait as context for the

105
00:04:30.371 --> 00:04:32.451
next response. A more important event can become a

106
00:04:32.451 --> 00:04:34.620
spoken interruption, according to configurable priority

107
00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.564
thresholds. There is also a cooldown around interruptions, so

108
00:04:37.564 --> 00:04:39.768
repeated reports can be deferred instead of repeatedly talking

109
00:04:39.768 --> 00:04:40.473
over the same exchange.

110
00:04:41.558 --> 00:04:43.660
Permission questions return through Converse as well.

111
00:04:44.207 --> 00:04:46.274
The background worker can request a decision, but the

112
00:04:46.274 --> 00:04:48.288
person hears and answers through the conversation interface.

113
00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.485
This keeps one recognizable place for human interaction while

114
00:04:51.485 --> 00:04:53.128
the internal agent loop handles its own state.

115
00:04:53.746 --> 00:04:55.724
The useful design idea is a clear boundary between

116
00:04:55.724 --> 00:04:57.526
doing background work and managing the conversation.

117
00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.248
Imagine an example conversation about simplifying a settings

118
00:05:03.248 --> 00:05:05.433
screen. You say, let us keep the main controls

119
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:07.880
visible and put advanced options behind a separate panel.

120
00:05:08.426 --> 00:05:10.723
Converse transcribes that request and asks the response model

121
00:05:10.723 --> 00:05:13.341
for an answer. You hear a suggestion, and you

122
00:05:13.341 --> 00:05:15.468
can immediately refine it with another spoken turn.

123
00:05:16.446 --> 00:05:18.988
Meanwhile, a transcript update reaches the background agent.

124
00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:21.700
A useful state report might capture the emerging decision:

125
00:05:22.171 --> 00:05:24.431
keep everyday controls prominent and group advanced settings

126
00:05:24.431 --> 00:05:26.729
together. That report can inform the next response.

127
00:05:27.445 --> 00:05:29.799
This is an illustration of the information flow, rather

128
00:05:29.799 --> 00:05:31.607
than a claim that the agent has already edited

129
00:05:31.607 --> 00:05:32.059
a file.

130
00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.221
If the background loop needs a decision, the question

131
00:05:35.221 --> 00:05:36.464
can come back through Converse.

132
00:05:37.085 --> 00:05:39.438
If you change direction, the next transcript update carries

133
00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:42.696
that change forward. Separating these responsibilities makes the

134
00:05:42.696 --> 00:05:45.388
interaction easier to follow: one loop speaks with you,

135
00:05:45.689 --> 00:05:47.365
and another keeps track of the context it has

136
00:05:47.365 --> 00:05:47.665
been given.

137
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.572
Provider flexibility creates a second problem: knowing which

138
00:05:53.572 --> 00:05:56.622
choices are valid. Korina centralizes provider metadata in a

139
00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.233
backend registry and exposes it through a capabilities endpoint.

140
00:05:59.671 --> 00:06:01.735
The frontend reads that contract to decide which provider

141
00:06:01.735 --> 00:06:03.653
options appear and whether a base address can be

142
00:06:03.653 --> 00:06:03.953
edited.

143
00:06:04.796 --> 00:06:07.502
For managed local providers, changing the response provider can

144
00:06:07.502 --> 00:06:09.916
trigger the appropriate service lifecycle before the model list

145
00:06:09.916 --> 00:06:13.097
refreshes. Model capability information also helps filter the

146
00:06:13.097 --> 00:06:15.620
audio model picker. A text only model should not

147
00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.961
quietly appear as an ordinary choice for audio transcription

148
00:06:17.961 --> 00:06:20.484
when the system already knows it cannot accept that

149
00:06:20.484 --> 00:06:20.784
input.

150
00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.189
Capability declarations can still meet a different reality at

151
00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:26.730
the endpoint. Korina therefore includes an audio probe path

152
00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.089
that can recognize an unsupported audio request, fall back

153
00:06:29.089 --> 00:06:30.351
to whisper, and remember the result.

154
00:06:31.064 --> 00:06:33.843
The broader lesson is practical: configuration describes intent,

155
00:06:34.337 --> 00:06:36.348
while a real request reveals what the selected model

156
00:06:36.348 --> 00:06:37.738
and serving stack can actually do.

157
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.023
The repository expresses these responsibilities in its structure.

158
00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.855
A FastAPI application factory composes route modules.

159
00:06:46.194 --> 00:06:48.889
Service modules own speech recognition, model discovery, provider

160
00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:50.805
management, response generation, and agent behavior.

161
00:06:51.348 --> 00:06:53.433
The browser frontend uses native JavaScript modules for recording,

162
00:06:53.925 --> 00:06:55.942
voice activity detection, playback, settings, and coordination.

163
00:06:57.036 --> 00:06:58.680
There is also a Converse channel protocol.

164
00:06:59.409 --> 00:07:02.240
An adapter implements sending, streaming, cancellation, and health

165
00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.273
behavior, with common request and response types.

166
00:07:04.765 --> 00:07:06.053
A registry selects the active channel.

167
00:07:06.562 --> 00:07:08.545
That gives future integrations a defined place to connect

168
00:07:08.917 --> 00:07:10.968
instead of requiring every conversation route to know each

169
00:07:10.968 --> 00:07:11.494
agent implementation.

170
00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.961
The distinction between a contract and a complete integration

171
00:07:14.961 --> 00:07:17.825
is important here. The repository includes a Hermes stub

172
00:07:17.825 --> 00:07:19.876
that demonstrates the channel interface and returns canned

173
00:07:19.876 --> 00:07:22.605
responses. A working external agent client still needs real

174
00:07:22.605 --> 00:07:25.284
integration code. The architecture creates a path for replacement;

175
00:07:25.928 --> 00:07:27.809
each replacement must supply the behavior behind that interface.

176
00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.710
To run the intended local stack, you need the

177
00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.731
Korina backend and browser assets, a recognition backend, a

178
00:07:35.731 --> 00:07:37.373
response model service, and a speech endpoint.

179
00:07:38.612 --> 00:07:40.271
The repository provides startup helpers and user service

180
00:07:40.271 --> 00:07:42.576
definitions for the Korina application and Kokoro streaming

181
00:07:42.576 --> 00:07:45.440
server. Provider activation manages supported local response

182
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.208
services separately.

183
00:07:47.164 --> 00:07:48.909
The settings interface ties those parts together.

184
00:07:49.503 --> 00:07:51.858
You can select recognition settings, a response provider and

185
00:07:51.858 --> 00:07:53.970
model, a speech endpoint and voice, and the background

186
00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.110
agent configuration. Runtime settings are stored locally, while a

187
00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.658
tracked example configuration documents the shape without bundling

188
00:07:59.658 --> 00:08:01.124
your own runtime state into the source tree.

189
00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.577
For a useful first session, verify the entire path:

190
00:08:04.926 --> 00:08:07.772
microphone input, a readable transcript, an actual model reply,

191
00:08:08.209 --> 00:08:10.706
and audible playback. A backend process being alive proves

192
00:08:10.706 --> 00:08:11.754
only one part of that chain.

193
00:08:12.487 --> 00:08:14.915
Korina has health and regression tooling, and composite readiness

194
00:08:14.915 --> 00:08:17.167
across the full stack remains an explicit area for

195
00:08:17.167 --> 00:08:17.674
further improvement.

196
00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:22.643
Korina already contains the pieces for a local voice

197
00:08:22.643 --> 00:08:25.795
conversation, modular backend and frontend code, provider rules,

198
00:08:26.203 --> 00:08:28.343
audio capability handling, and an agent reporting path.

199
00:08:28.784 --> 00:08:30.972
The repository also contains automated checks and separate live

200
00:08:30.972 --> 00:08:33.292
regression tooling. Those are useful engineering foundations,

201
00:08:33.667 --> 00:08:35.121
while the quality of a session still depends on

202
00:08:35.121 --> 00:08:36.395
the actual models and hardware you choose.

203
00:08:37.351 --> 00:08:39.916
The documentation names several remaining directions: a fuller

204
00:08:39.916 --> 00:08:42.709
execution runtime, stronger readiness across services, portable

205
00:08:42.709 --> 00:08:44.947
installation paths, and richer model capability evidence.

206
00:08:45.469 --> 00:08:47.691
External agent support also needs concrete clients behind the

207
00:08:47.691 --> 00:08:50.173
shared channel contract. These are useful distinctions when

208
00:08:50.173 --> 00:08:52.084
evaluating what the project does today and what its

209
00:08:52.084 --> 00:08:53.260
architecture is intended to support.

210
00:08:54.135 --> 00:08:56.762
For a contributor, the boundaries suggest focused places to

211
00:08:56.762 --> 00:08:58.867
work. Improve recognition without rewriting playback.

212
00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:00.760
Add an agent adapter behind the protocol.

213
00:09:01.315 --> 00:09:03.648
Make provider discovery more accurate, or make setup easier

214
00:09:03.648 --> 00:09:05.917
to reproduce. Each improvement can strengthen the whole

215
00:09:05.917 --> 00:09:07.809
conversation when its inputs and outputs stay clear.

216
00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.779
The central idea of Korina is a conversation layer

217
00:09:12.779 --> 00:09:14.551
that can stay useful as the components around it

218
00:09:14.551 --> 00:09:16.268
change. Your microphone becomes text.

219
00:09:16.716 --> 00:09:18.469
A response model turns that text into an answer.

220
00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:20.240
A speech service makes the answer audible.

221
00:09:20.992 --> 00:09:23.712
Alongside it, the background agent receives context and returns

222
00:09:23.712 --> 00:09:25.751
information through a separate coordination path.

223
00:09:26.667 --> 00:09:29.656
Keeping those responsibilities explicit helps explain the project.

224
00:09:30.211 --> 00:09:31.410
Converse owns the human interaction.

225
00:09:31.983 --> 00:09:33.612
The agent owns its background state and reports.

226
00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.798
Provider contracts describe the available connections, and

227
00:09:36.798 --> 00:09:38.928
adapters define where another implementation can fit.

228
00:09:39.555 --> 00:09:41.739
Together, they form a foundation for experimenting with voice

229
00:09:41.739 --> 00:09:42.777
driven work on your own machine.

230
00:09:43.764 --> 00:09:46.107
If you explore the repository, start with the readme

231
00:09:46.107 --> 00:09:48.503
and architecture pages, then follow one spoken turn through

232
00:09:48.503 --> 00:09:49.827
the browser modules and backend routes.

233
00:09:50.417 --> 00:09:51.704
That path connects the code to the experience.

234
00:09:52.563 --> 00:09:54.423
Korina is a project about making those parts work

235
00:09:54.423 --> 00:09:56.748
together, so a natural conversation can become a practical

236
00:09:56.748 --> 00:09:57.713
way to work with an agent.
